    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d649">

                <pb facs="0837" n="743" sameAs="frus:frus1924v02/pg743"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">867.113/33</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in Great
                        Britain</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Kellogg</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1924-04-15"><hi rendition="#italic">April 15,
                        1924</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 155</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: With reference to the Department’s
                    instruction No. 1053 of December 10, 1923,<anchor n="43" xml:id="d649-f1"/>
                    there is enclosed, for your confidential information, a copy of a note from the
                    British Embassy dated April 5, 1924<anchor n="44" xml:id="d649-f2"/> with
                    reference to the removal by the British Government of the embargo on the export
                    of arms and munitions from Great Britain to Turkey. The Department has
                    acknowledged the receipt of this note without comment.</p>
                <p>The Department perceives no reason for altering at the present time the policy
                    set forth in its note of December 6, 1923 to the British Embassy, a copy of
                    which was enclosed with the instruction referred to above.</p>
                <p>I am [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Charles E. Hughes</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
